+# CONR after-action correspondence and the Arnold interview crosswalk
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review. B129 / PD01–PD04. Four PDFs extracted from the public mirror of the January 2009 Commission release provide two facsimile copies of the after-action correspondence, a staff question outline and an identified interview transcript. The source stamps connect **NCT0067550–NCT0067563** to the 2002 Filson transcript. **NCT0025873–NCT0025919**, the separately listed CONR After Action Report, remains an index entry awaiting the report body and delivery evidence. [Archive item](https://archive.org/details/911datasets) · [DoD index reader](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556058/DM-B3-DOD-2-of-2-Fdr-All-Document-Indexes-in-File-272)
+
+## The interview index identifies a specific 14-page transcript
+
+The Steve Dunne DoD index reader describes **Interview: Major General Arnold**, **Unclas**, **CONR**, **22,2**, and **20-Feb-04** on reader text line **457**. Its starting stamp is rendered **5cT0067550** in the flattened reader text (with preceding mark-like characters). The PDF page face reads **NCT0067550**. The rightmost index date and the transcript's stated interview date are separate fields. [DoD index reader](https://www.scribd.com/document/15907260/SD-B2-DOD-1-of-2-Fdr-All-Document-Indexes-in-Folder-Additional-in-DM-B3-DOD-and-T2-B22-DOD-739)
+
+The exact ZIP member is **T8 B22 Filson Materials Fdr- Larry Arnold Interview- Typed Notes.pdf**. Its physical p. **4**, lines **1–6**, labels the speaker pair and Tape Side A and gives **September 11, 2002**. Its first stamp is **NCT0067550**. Physical p. **17**, lines **30–38**, ends Tape Side B and shows **NCT0067563**. The 14 physical pages from 4 through 17 have 13 consecutive text-layer stamps and one stamp visible only on the checked page image. The clipped bottom on p. **15** was read as **NCT0067561**. Physical p. **27**, line **12**, separately displays **9/11/02** in the typed notes. The index's **20-Feb-04**, the transcript date and the separate **2/3/04** Commission interview heading are preserved. The match identifies the stamped transcript within a 38-page compilation. The raw recording, transcript production history and original delivery cover remain open. [Public ZIP source and exact member](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip) · [Separate Commission interview release](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048-doc34.pdf#page=1)
+
+## Preserve the different descriptions of CONR after-action work
+
+| Record | What it records |
+| --- | --- |
+| **May 23, 2003 public hearing**, HTML raw line **499** | Arnold refers to how things worked “in terms of the after-action reports” in a discussion of broader response arrangements. He supplies no title, author or office for those reports in this passage. [Official Commission transcript, UNT mirror](https://govinfo.library.unt.edu/911/archive/hearing2/9-11Commission_Hearing_2003-05-23.htm) |
+| **October 14, 2003 DoD memorandum**, Marcus member **268** pp. **1–2** | Reports that NORAD had submitted several after-action reports to DoD and says delivery to the Commission was expected by **October 27**. The same paragraph prints **9/1/01** for personnel on duty. The surrounding packet addresses September 11. Preserve the printed date as it stands. Keep the memo's separate September 12 delivery claim for request 4 items 6 and 7 apart from the expected October delivery. [Correspondence reader](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556037/DM-B3-DOD-1-of-2-Fdr-Memos-and-Correspondence-Re-Document-Production-NORAD-After-Action-Reports-NOIWON-Conference-Call-268) |
+| **October 24, 2003 meeting summary**, member **268** p. **5**, lines **8–19, 29–37** | Reports that Commission staff had an interim report and slides and had found no indication of a final report. The DoD summary also records staff's stated belief that nobody was hiding documents and their concern that searches may have missed responsive material. These are reported statements of staff views. The actual titles and delivered enclosures remain unverified. [Correspondence reader](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556037/DM-B3-DOD-1-of-2-Fdr-Memos-and-Correspondence-Re-Document-Production-NORAD-After-Action-Reports-NOIWON-Conference-Call-268) |
+| **February 3, 2004 Commission handwritten notes**, ISCAP **2011-048 doc 34**, physical p. **19**, first bullet | Reads “No after action report done by CONR,” with timeline material below. The page image carries that wording. It is interview note-taking, with no verbatim speaker attribution for the bullet. The same page records a denial of an effort to sanitize the timeline and a qualification about awareness of a decision to make FAA look better. [NARA release, p. 19](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2011-048-doc34.pdf#page=19) |
+| **CONR staff question outline**, p. **1**, lines **9–11**, and p. **3**, lines **1–6** | Asks for confirmation that no after-action report existed and asks why. A separate passage asks for the source of **0924** and for actual information about Flight 77. The questions are evidence of intended follow-up. The outline supplies no answer. [Public ZIP, Team 8 Box 19 member](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip) |
+| **March 16, 2004 DoD and NORAD correspondence**, member **267** pp. **3–5**, member **268** pp. **11–13** | DoD counsel applied a military-report definition and pointed to weekly delivery indexes. NORAD counsel distinguished a single final comprehensive report from a series of after-action reports it said had been supplied. The file includes an enclosure face and a continuation bearing the signatory's name. These two copies reproduce the same correspondence chain. [Previously published reader review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/3324e3ae36db9189a79658db51ef04acb6663219/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-march16-after-action-correspondence-2026-11-08/README.md) |
+| **June 17, 2004 Commission hearing**, printed and physical pp. **41 and 68** | Ben-Veniste described after-action-report efforts and contemporaneous communications. Asked about a report and the logs, Arnold said he did not recall doing an after-action report and described efforts to capture takeoff and reaction times. Keep that qualification and the scope of the question. [Commission hearing, p. 41](https://www.9-11commission.gov/archive/hearing12/9-11Commission_Hearing_2004-06-17.pdf#page=41) · [P. 68](https://www.9-11commission.gov/archive/hearing12/9-11Commission_Hearing_2004-06-17.pdf#page=68) |
+
+The **NCT0025873–NCT0025919** row is labelled **After Action Report**, **CONR**, **1,10**, **12-Sep-03**. Its label and the February notes' negative statement are preserved side by side. The row provides no title-specific event description. Neither the October 24 interim-report reference nor the March 16 statement identifies that range as an enclosure. An original with title, authorship, date and scope plus the dated weekly delivery and Commission intake records would test the relationship. Retain the separate Senate answer about DoD review and Myers's position on operational discipline. [DoD index](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556058/DM-B3-DOD-2-of-2-Fdr-All-Document-Indexes-in-File-272) · [Senate questions 40–41](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=184)
+
+## Source representations and tests
+
+The October 14 fax header reads **OCT-14-2003** on the page image, whereas the embedded text gives **OCT-14-2083**. On the two March 16 continuation faces (267 p. 5 and 268 p. 13), the visible time is **18:05**. The extracted text gives **18 = 05** and **13=05**, respectively. The date portion is clipped. Preserve the image and text readings as separate observations. The five corresponding page pairs from the two PDFs are unequal at the default PyMuPDF raster-pixel level. Selected passages and signature blocks identify corresponding letters. The image test supplies no chronology of copying or reason for the pixel differences.
+
+Seven held bodies and the reused index hash verified. The four exact archive members passed the byte-range, member name, ZIP CRC and size checks. The six PDFs total **224** physical pages. Reading covered the cited passages, selected rendered pages, 26 fixed PDF text anchors, one raw HTML line, and 14 transcript stamp positions. PyMuPDF reported a structure-tree warning for the June hearing during the wider scan. The cited text anchors and selected rendered pages were readable. The first ISCAP retrieval exceeded the local acquisition cap, then the reported **14,037,845** length was confirmed and the full body verified under a 15 MB cap. No underlying CONR report was identified among the acquired members.
+
+- [ ] **CONR report and delivery.** Obtain the original **NCT0025873–NCT0025919** report, exact September and October delivery indexes, Commission intake and the source supporting the October meeting summary. Compare all after-action titles, dates, producing offices and enclosure spans. Seek the actual CONR question responses and the four logs referred to at the June hearing.
+- [ ] **Interview and chronology.** Seek the 2002 Filson recording and delivery cover, the original 2004 interview record and any separate response to the February after-action requests. Check exact edition, agency and Commission controls, and time or reference-source claims before making operational joins.
+- [ ] **FAA PD01–PD04.** The actual corrected May 2003 FAA answer and acknowledgment, signed action/no-action decision, recommendation disposition and applicable policy and routing remain open. The newly acquired records establish specific custody and scope questions in the parallel DoD chain. [FAA case recommendations](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
